ou start doing voiceover powerpoints  that in order to make the class  consistent you have to stick with that  and never do anything else you can do  whatever works for you so there's almost  you could say a sort of hierarchy going  from the bottom all the way up in terms  of engagement for your videos and so  let's talk about some of the different  options voice over PowerPoint or if  you're using a Mac of course slides or  the Google equivalent as well will work  for you that is is sort of the the most  basic thing that you can do it's not  very engaging but it does provide you  know content for your students and they  get to hear your voice which is going to  be important for them another thing that  you might want to consider doing a  second format would be screen recording  and so like an Apple you would use  QuickTime for that office 365 for  Windows users has its own variation of  that if you have Camtasia you can do  that so you can show them you doing  something on the screen and talking over  that a lot of YouTube tutorials look  like that and so depending on what  you're teaching that might be a good way  to do things  another thing that I've done like with  my critical thinking videos in the past  is slides and you just you know print  and export the slides you can do it from  PowerPoint you can do it from from  whatever other format you're using and  you just drop them into your video  editor and then you can use your video  editor to do a picture in picture of you  in the bottom or maybe at the top going  over that the one thing you have to do  if you're going to use that format is  make sure your slide doesn't have  information that's getting blocked out  by your lovely face and you know the  image of you leading the students  through that I myself prefer to use  recorded videos like you're seeing right  now me talking into the camera or in  front of the Troc board  you can use all sorts of other things  we're going to get to that in a minute  instead of a chalkboard but that's  probably the most engaging for students  when they can actually see you their  instructor not just your face and not  just like a portion of your body but a  good bit of you moving around talking in  the way that you normally would students  do in fact really respond to the human  face and figure the one thing I would  avoid the you know and they were quite  popular years back so many of you  remember these those animated videos  where you put in text and then in a kind  of robot voice a pig and a rabbit talk  to each other not engaging at all  I wouldn't use those for students but  any of the other formats I think would  be quite useful for you  another format consideration that you're  probably going to want to have in mind  is where you're going to upload the  video once you've actually produced it  and there you've got a number of  different choices as an educator so one  thing is you can use your own personal  YouTube or Vimeo channel that's not a  bad thing to do not least because  YouTube will provide you with closed  captioning which can be useful for your  students who are hearing-impaired or  have other you know reasons for wanting  to see text and and it will provide them  with transcripts it's not a hundred  percent accurate accent sometimes trip  it up but it's it's you know decent and  it's gotten a lot better in recent years  many institutions if you're using Google  suite will already have a YouTube  channel right there created for you  within your institution so I have for  example a Milwaukee Institute of Art and  Design YouTube channel which I use only  for that miwaukee Area Technical College  also does that so you probably have that  if your if your institution as Google  suite another similar thing would be  called Tura  and both of those who have the advantage  of again of being able to provide closed  caption and then you might also consider  directly uploading your video into  whatever course management system you're  using that's like blackboard or canvas  or Sakai or Moodle because all of them  can upload fairly short videos and you  know depending on the bandwidth maybe  even longer videos you want to check to  see what that is maybe talk to your IT  person or you know you can also find  that in the course management system the  one disadvantage of that is that you're  not going to get closed captioning now  you know in the middle of a crisis  people aren't going to be that concerned  about a DEA compliance but it is nice  for students to have that so you might  want to consider you know your  institutional YouTube or personal  YouTube or Kaltura or something along  those lines  let's talk now about the actual  production and I'm not going to go  through how to do the video editing  software or anything like that there's  all sorts of tutorials out there for you  I will say this there's a learning curve  for any one of them I use iMovie but  there's you know all sorts of other  variations any of them are fine for what  you're gonna be doing just expect it to  take a little while what I'm gonna talk  about now is more about the production  on the shooting site like what I'm doing  right now talking to you so the very  first thing that I want to point out is  you're doing video which means that you  can do multiple takes take the pressure  off of yourself you don't need to get it  right the first time I sometimes get in  front of the chalkboard and shoot for  three minutes of me starting out a video  and not being happy with how I started  it or losing my train of thought and  then I just you know start again one  thing you might want to consider doing  if you're shooting at all like that is  don't stop the camera just keep going  and do this put a wave for yourself so  you know that at that point in time you  had you know some sort of glitch or a  mistake or brain fart or whatever you're  going to call it and that that's where  you want to pick up from and that's  perfectly fine  that's what editing is for you can take  pauses if you need to take a 20-minute  break go ahead and do that you can take  the pressure off of yourself so that's  that's the very first thing that we want  to think about second is a lot of people  are worried do I need to like write out  an entire script before I start shooting  and you can do that I mean right now  what I'm doing with this video I've got  some notes here and I'm going off of  those notes most of my videos that I  produce i've got stuff on the chalkboard  I use it as a rough guide from my own  thinking but most of what I'm doing  whether I'm you know lecturing online or  going face-to-face or even providing  lectures you know the pain lectures here  and there is going off of notes at most  or sometimes just what's in my head  that's me  a lot of you can do that probably more  of you than you think can but some  people might want to have a script and  it's perfectly fine to use a script as a  matter of fact if that makes you feel  more confident do that because that's  going to produce higher-quality video in  terms of content for your students and  that's what everything here is about  another thing that I think is really  helpful as you're either you know  scripting it or thinking out what you're  gonna say is anticipating where the pain  points for your students are where are  they going to get confused what  terminology is gonna trip them up what  questions are they likely to have you  might actually reserve some time at the  end of the video and you could like you  know again just take a pause and then  come back to that for likely questions  so you know when I'm shooting a video  for example on Kant's categorical  imperative the first formulation man  students are gonna get that mixed up and  so when I do a video on it I need to  think about okay how are they going to  go astray and how can I explain that to  them and you can do that in your own  field that you're the expert you  understand those things and presumably  you have some experience in seeing where  students are gonna need a little bit of  extra help so you can build that into  your video a few other things that I  would say you know you can use a  blackboard if you want to I'm kind of  old school that way some people like to  use a whiteboard or a flip chart one of  those paper things you flip over any of  those are perfectly fine you don't have  to have that though you could also  provide them with a write-up of your  video or slides if there's some content  that you want them to have it doesn't  all have to be in there in every single  part of the video it's a-okay  to do it with you know what works for  you and what you think your students  need the last thing that I'm going to  say about the actual production part and  this is very important  sometimes educators buy into this this  thing that went around it's essentially  a meme  among educational experts that we call  the five-minute rule  the idea was that videos in order to  engage the you know somewhat attention  deprived and and technology oriented  students of that then as I'm the future  because this has been said for twenty  years videos have to be only five  minutes long that is complete nonsense  students and other people will watch an  hour-long video if the content is really  good and engaging now I'm not saying  shoot an hour-long video I would say the  sweet spot is between 10 and 20 minutes  shorter videos can be useful but you  can't convey as much information now can  you think about if I decided to make  this only 5 minutes how much you'd be  missing out on all right it's perfectly  fine for you to shoot videos that are 10  minutes 15 minutes 20 minutes  you know half an hour all of those they  will work for your students students can  always take a pause when they want to  the key is the content has to be  interesting engaging worth watching and  again you're the experts so you can  figure out how to make your content fit  that bill I know that I have videos that  are an hour and a half long and people  watch them from the beginning all the  way to the end not everybody does of  course and I wouldn't suggest using  those four classes necessarily but  people will do that so you can dispense  with any worries about the five-minute  rule all of these things are worth  keeping in mind I said that that was the  last thing but I will say one other  thing in your videos don't try to be  somebody else your students already know  you as their instructor that they've  seen face to face for a while  just be yourself be conversational it  doesn't have to be perfect you are the  person that they are tuning in to help  them with whatever it is that you're  studying in that class so just keep that  in mind and the students will watch it  and be very grateful that you created  that material for them  once you've created your videos they  have to have a place to go a home to  live and that is most likely going to be  the course management system that you're  using for your now online course so it  could be blackboard Sakai moodle  desire2learn canvas Schoology all of  them and there's there's many others as  well you'll only need to know one of  them and that's the one that you're  putting things into and each of them has  a way to incorporate videos so if you  like put your videos into YouTube or the  institutional YouTube channel there's a  link and there's also an embed code and  you want to get familiar with how to  find those and you'll be able to put  those into the course management system  and you don't have to get fancy with it  you don't have to you know create you  know long sequences that are perfect or  anything like that you just have to have  your students able to access them so  even just writing the name of the video  and then the you know the link that is  perfectly fine so long as students can  access it there are probably tutorials  for any one of the systems that you're  using that can help you if you want to  embed it you know so they can see the  video playing within the system you can  also contact your campus IT departments  even though campuses are closed they are  still providing at least in most cases  some guidance and help you know for  people that are now trying to get fully  online the last thing that I do want to  say about this and I should have  mentioned this a little bit earlier this  can be a good sign you know you can  always switch things up in a video make  sure when you're uploading your videos  whether it's you know in in YouTube or  an institutional YouTube or directly  into the course management system make  sure that you have an accurate title you  don't have to worry so much about having  a nice thumbnail or anything like that  because you're not worried about search  engine optimization or getting lots of  views  you're worried about your students  understanding what the video is about so  those are the things that have to do  with with you know you might say the  implementation the incorporation of  videos into the actual class that you're  working on the last thing that I'll say  when you post new video content maybe  send out a message to your students  saying hey there's some videos for you  to check out because you know students  are going to need reminders from time to  time  I think there's a lot of common concerns  and worries that that first-time video  producers now perhaps in many cases  first-time online teachers will have  about this whole process and so you know  at the end of this video I want to take  a little time to try to allay some fears  and anxieties if possible based on my  experience and on what the research  tells us about you know lecture capture  video production all those sorts of  things so the first question that you  can ask yourself and probably a lot of  you envisioning shooting videos have  already done this and perhaps some of  you engage in some negative self-talk  about this is the video I'm gonna  produce or the videos that I'm going to  produce is it good enough and the answer  in almost every case is gonna be yes it  is good enough because you've all heard  that that phrase from back in the you  know early days of the internet content  is king content matters the most are you  able to convey information effectively  to your students good everything else is  gravy if it sounds great that's that's  great you want it to sound at least good  enough that students can make out what's  being said but if there's a little bit  of background hiss nobody's gonna ding  you on that does the lighting have to be  perfect absolutely not  you know so long as they can actually  see you or see the track board or  whatever it else it is that you're using  so long as the slides you know if you're  if you're doing PowerPoint stuff look  decent they don't have to be fancy it's  good enough so you're just doing the  basic bare-bones later on if you really  like doing this you can get more  advanced but right now what's important  is producing content for your students  so that they they can wrap their heads  around the the things that you're  studying in your class after the  disruptions that we're experiencing and  through a time of crisis so good enough  is good enough another key thing I would  say I mentioned you know being yourself  your students already have a rapport  or with you you don't need to think  about you know shooting videos that are  gonna somehow connect you to viewers  that have never seen you you're talking  to your students so not only should you  you know be yourself but you can expect  them to already relate to you you can  refer to things that you did before in  class and and you can use videos to kind  of build a sense of community students  are not going to be upset with you for  doing videos they are gonna be happy and  grateful that you're actually taking the  time to prepare materials for them that  lets them see a human face and voice  somebody who's concerned about them both  their health and their education so you  know smile you know we are in the middle  of a crisis but that doesn't mean that  you know everything has to be doom and  gloom at least in videos and you know  you might begin your videos this is a  suggestion from my wife who's always got  great advice about this you might  express some hopes that they're doing  well and you might close on something  like that as well another concern I  think that that some people have is  worries about being judged or evaluated  on the basis of their videos nobody is  going to be judging you or evaluating  you you know not not students not  administrators again we're in the middle  of a crisis the key is to make education  possible and to do so you know in an  engaging manner a well informed manner  it doesn't have to be highly produced if  you if you say um and AH like I do all  the time nobody is gonna hold that  against you this is for classes this is  not stuff that you're putting out there  on the internet for everybody  particularly trolls to go after you on  the last thing that I'll say in this  this this part is one thing to watch out  for is you know as you get excited about  the material that you're talking about  there is a human tendency to start  talking faster and faster and faster  that also happens when we're nervous or  anxious so you know  to pace your delivery a bit you know you  don't have to worry over much about what  you're wearing I mean if you want to  like dress up for class you certainly  can but there's no inherent necessity so  hopefully this has been helpful for you  has given you some ideas about how you  would go on and produce videos it's  gonna be a different sort of set of  processes for everybody because you've  got different software and hardware and  different material that you're  presenting but the basics are the same  you find something that works for you  whether it's an iPhone your computer's  camera another camera you try to make  sure that it's decent enough you know  picture and sound quality and lighting  but it doesn't have to be perfect and  you teach you teach your material you  teach it to your students you don't have  to worry about trying to pack it all in  in five minutes  dispense with that nonsense five minutes  rule it can be ten minutes twenty  minutes you know you can upload it to in  a lot of different ways you can edit it  in a lot of different ways if it's not  perfect if it's not beautiful that's  okay what matters is getting it done and  you've got lots of opportunities because  of the way video processing works to do  it you know multiple takes if you want  to if you're not happy with the first  take do it again  you're gonna get better and better at  this as time goes on there's a learning  curve so I encourage all of you to jump  in you can ask me questions in the  comments and I'll be happy to address  them and you know we'll see about maybe  shooting some other videos that are a  little bit more focused about particular  issues you know like why you would pick  this format or that format but but for  now just start start earning away and  see whether you can get some things out  there for your students you know maybe  by the end of the week  you
 

